class:: Ecostoch
summary:: Predator-prey population synthesis
related:: Classes/Limcycle, Classes/Lifestoch, Classes/Oscnet
categories:: UGens>Generators>Chaotic

description::

A coupled Lotka–Volterra predator-prey system integrated per sample with RK4, used as a
sound generator: the prey population drives the carrier amplitude (AM) and the predator
population drives the FM index, so the ecological orbit becomes the modulation envelope.
An optional demographic-noise term (fluctuations scaling with the square root of the
population) makes the cycle genuinely stochastic rather than a clean periodic orbit.

The system is time-scaled so the linearized cycle sits at code::rate:: Hz; equilibria are
code::decay/interaction:: (prey) and code::growth/interaction:: (predator). Three outputs:
code::[signal, preyCV, predCV]:: — tap the CVs to modulate other UGens.

classmethods::

method:: ar
argument:: rate
Frequency of the ecological cycle (Hz), sub-audio to audio. Default 4.
argument:: growth
Prey growth rate. Default 1.
argument:: decay
Predator death rate. Default 1.
argument:: interaction
Predation coupling strength. Default 1.
argument:: carrierFreq
Carrier oscillator frequency (Hz). Default 220.
argument:: fmIndex
FM depth driven by the predator population. Default 2.
argument:: noise
Demographic-noise amount (stochastic population fluctuations). Default 0.
argument:: oversample
Integration sub-steps per sample, 1–32. Default 4.
argument:: mul
argument:: add

examples::
code::
// the ecological cycle as a slow FM/AM envelope
{ Ecostoch.ar(4, carrierFreq: 220, fmIndex: 3)[0] * 0.3 ! 2 }.play;

// rate up into the audio range -> the orbit itself becomes a tone
{ Ecostoch.ar(MouseX.kr(2, 200, 1), carrierFreq: 110, fmIndex: 4)[0] * 0.2 ! 2 }.play;

// demographic noise breaks the clean cycle into a living, drifting one
{ Ecostoch.ar(6, 1, 1, 1, 200, 3, MouseX.kr(0, 0.4))[0] * 0.2 ! 2 }.play;

// interaction strength reshapes the orbit (spikier predation peaks)
{ Ecostoch.ar(8, 1, 1, MouseX.kr(0.3, 4), 160, 5)[0] * 0.2 ! 2 }.play;

// use the population CVs to modulate other UGens
{ var e = Ecostoch.ar(3); SinOsc.ar(300 + (e[1] * 200)) * (e[2] * 0.5 + 0.5) * 0.2 ! 2 }.play;
::
